    "result_text": "The full test suite passes: **281 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field on owner creation following the codebase's established pattern for computed owner fields (like `namesakeCount` and `sharesHousehold`).\n\n**The rule:** on `POST /api/owners`, a new owner's `locality` is `\"local\"` if the owner's city is the *single* most common city among owners that already exist at that moment, otherwise `\"remote\"`. A tie for most common city means there is no single most common city, so `\"remote\"`. The owner being created isn't yet saved, so it's never counted against itself. Cities are compared case-insensitively.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **`AssignOwnerLocality.java`** (new) \u2014 computes the locality from `ownerRepository.findAll()` before save, mirroring the existing `AssignOwner*` functions.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `assignLocality` into the pipeline between `assignSharesHousehold` and `save`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `locality` field (`@Column(name = \"locality\")`) with getter/setter.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `locality` property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name).\n- **schema.sql** for all four databases (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `locality VARCHAR(20)` column.\n\nThe provided `Cp18Tests` passes, and no existing tests regressed.",
